1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:12,850
Before processed microfilm becomes a preservation master, check four things: density, resolution, residual hypo and physical defects.

2
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,900
NARA's rule, 36 CFR 1238.14, is a useful benchmark.

3
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:23,930
First, background density, read with a transmission densitometer.

4
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:35,250
NARA recommends ranges by document type, from 1.3 to 1.5 for high-contrast print down to 0.7 to 0.85 for poor-contrast originals.

5
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,580
Base plus fog must not exceed 0.10.

6
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:40,530
Second, resolution.

7
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:46,530
Film an ISO 3334 test chart and read the smallest pattern you can resolve.

8
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:54,360
NARA asks for a Quality Index of five at the third generation, so legibility survives duplication.

9
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:58,800
Third, residual hypo, the fixer left after washing.

10
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:07,030
The limit is 0.014 grams per square metre, in line with ISO 18901, checked with a methylene blue test.

11
00:01:07,530 --> 00:01:17,340
Finally, inspect every frame on a light box for scratches and bad splices, log defects by roll, and store masters off-site in proper conditions.

12
